## cs-hist | History of computing | cs | 0 | - | cs-info
story 4 | The first computer bug was a real moth
- 1947: operators of the Harvard Mark II found a moth stuck in a relay and taped it into the logbook: "First actual case of bug being found."
- "Bug" for a fault predates it, but the moth is real and survives in the Smithsonian.
- *Grace Hopper popularised the story.*
> Grace Hopper built the first compiler

story 4 | Grace Hopper
- A US Navy rear admiral, she developed one of the first compilers in the early 1950s and drove the creation of COBOL.
- She handed out 30 cm lengths of wire to show what a nanosecond is: how far light travels in one.
- *"It's easier to ask forgiveness than it is to get permission."*
> Moore's law

explain 3 | Moore's law
- 1965: Gordon Moore observed transistors per chip were doubling roughly every year; in 1975 he revised it to every two years.
- It held, roughly, for about five decades.
- *It's an observation about engineering and economics, not a law of physics.*
> The web was invented at CERN

story 4 | Tim Berners-Lee invented the web to share physics papers
- 1989: at CERN, he proposed a system of linked documents. His boss wrote "Vague but exciting" on the proposal.
- The first website went live in 1991.
- *CERN released the web's technology royalty-free in 1993.*
> Next: programming ideas

## cs-prog | Programming ideas | cs | 0 | - | cs-hist
explain 3 | Recursion: a function that calls itself
- factorial(n) = n × factorial(n − 1), stopping at factorial(0) = 1.
- Every recursion needs a base case, or it runs forever (and overflows the stack).
- *"To understand recursion, you must first understand recursion."*
> Abstraction

explain 3 | Abstraction hides complexity
- You use a car's steering wheel without knowing how the rack and pinion work.
- Good code exposes simple interfaces and hides details behind them.
- *Every layer of computing, from transistors to apps, is built this way.*
> Version control

real 3 | Git was written in about two weeks
- 2005: Linus Torvalds wrote Git when the Linux project lost free access to its previous tool.
- It tracks every change, lets many people work in parallel, and merges their work.
- *Nearly all software development now uses it.*
> Off-by-one errors

explain 2 | The two hardest problems in computer science
- An old joke: cache invalidation, naming things, and off-by-one errors.
- Off-by-one: looping once too often or too few times, like counting fence posts instead of gaps.
- *A 10 m fence with posts every metre needs 11 posts.*
> Next: security

## cs-sec | Cybersecurity | cs | 0 | - | cs-prog
explain 3 | Most hacks start with people, not code
- Phishing emails trick people into giving passwords or running malware.
- Attackers impersonate bosses, banks and IT support.
- *Multi-factor authentication blocks most automated account takeovers.*
> Password length beats complexity

explain 4 | Why long passphrases win
- Each extra random character multiplies the number of guesses needed.
- Four random common words can beat a short password full of symbols.
- *Reusing passwords is the bigger danger: one breach unlocks everything.*
> Stuxnet

story 5 | Stuxnet: malware that broke centrifuges
- Discovered in 2010, it targeted industrial control systems at Iran's uranium enrichment plant.
- It made centrifuges spin destructively while showing operators normal readings.
- *Widely believed to be a US–Israeli operation; the first known cyberweapon to cause physical destruction.*
> Next: companies and how they started

## ec-firm | Business origins | econ | 0 | - | ec-pf
story 3 | Nintendo started by making playing cards in 1889
- It made hanafuda cards in Kyoto, then tried taxis, instant rice and "love hotels" before toys and video games.
- *Its first major video game hits came in the 1980s.*
> Coca-Cola was sold as a medicine

story 3 | Coca-Cola started as a patent medicine
- Pharmacist John Pemberton created it in 1886, marketed as a nerve tonic.
- Early versions contained a small amount of cocaine from coca leaves; it was removed by the early 1900s.
- *The leaf extract is still used, de-cocainised.*
> Amazon's name

quiz 3 | What was Amazon originally incorporated as, in 1994?
* Cadabra
o Bookworld
o Relentless
o Earth's Biggest Bookstore
= Bezos renamed it because a lawyer misheard "Cadabra" as "cadaver".
= He also liked "Relentless": relentless.com still redirects to Amazon.
> Next: economic history

## ec-hist | Economic history | econ | 0 | - | ec-firm
explain 4 | The Great Divergence
- Around 1800, living standards in the richest parts of Europe and China were broadly comparable, by some estimates.
- Industrialisation then let Western Europe pull dramatically ahead.
- *Historians still debate why it happened there first: coal, colonies, institutions, or chance.*
> Adam Smith's pin factory

explain 3 | Adam Smith's pin factory
- 1776: Smith described ten workers, each specialising in one step, making about 48,000 pins a day.
- One worker alone might make 20 at most.
- *Division of labour as the engine of productivity.*
> Keynes vs Hayek

explain 4 | Keynes vs Hayek
- Keynes: in a slump, governments should spend to replace missing demand.
- Hayek: government intervention distorts prices and creates bigger problems later.
- *Their 1930s debate still shapes economic policy arguments.*
> The Marshall Plan

explain 3 | The Marshall Plan
- 1948–1952: the US gave around $13 billion (over $150 billion today) to rebuild Western Europe.
- It aimed to restore economies and counter Soviet influence.
- *Its exact economic impact is debated, but it's widely seen as a success.*
> Economics track part two complete
